epublican field is fractured three ways under ranked-choice voting while Kreiss-Tomkins is the sole Democrat with a dominant fundraising lead. Markets price a Democratic win at 75-81% and the only recent poll shows a narrow Democratic edge, justifying a modest Democratic tilt despite tossup ratings from most analysts.", "watch": "If the trailing Republican candidates consolidate or drop out and their support transfers under ranked-choice tabulation, the race could quickly shift back toward the GOP.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Mike Dunleavy (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Alaska's governorship is open with Mike Dunleavy term-limited, and the race has become one of the cycle's most volatile. Four candidates are on the November ballot: Democrat Jonathan Kreiss-Tomkins faces three Republicans — Treg Taylor, Bernadette Wilson, and Dave Bronson — under the state's ranked-choice system. Wilson has publicly urged her Republican rivals to drop out, and headlines describe a surge for the Democrat as the GOP fractures.\n\nKreiss-Tomkins has raised $2.2 million, far outpacing Taylor's $880,000, though Taylor holds more cash on hand. Most expert raters call the race a tossup after Cook shifted it from Lean Republican, but prediction markets price the Democrat at 75-81% to win. The lone recent poll shows a narrow Democratic lead. A divided Republican field gives the Democrat the edge, though consolidation could change the picture.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.6825, "margin": 2.46}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7223, "margin": 3.0}]}